this goat made a hearty meal for a  cheetah last night but there's even  enough left to feed the village this  evening  max muyamburuku and his team have come  to the village where it was killed in  the muduva nyangana conservancy they log  all events concerning wild animals here  previously hunters they were setting  snail for leipert  and tea for hyena if it kills a  goat or calf they will put their snails  so that they can kill that aina or  leopard  this is no longer allowed but poaching  is still a widespread problem especially  with wire snares  the rangers see this all too often  that is  another challenge that we are facing  some  are not really conserving but due to  hunger they are doing it so that they  can feed themselves  the conservancy is part of casa tfca the  kavango zambezi trans frontier  conservation area  casa is an international project  spanning namibia and its neighboring  countries it's the largest protected  area in africa more than seventeen  hundred people live here most of them  farmers like licoro cashera  things have changed here since we became  part of the casa program  since then we've lost more crops because  the population of the wild animals has  grown  and our cows have less land to graze on  because the wild animals need to graze  too there's more competition  but the farmers are compensated if  elephants destroy their crops  there are also hunting lodges in the  park trophy hunting brings in a lot of  money it's still an important source of  income for the community the hunters  don't want to talk on camera  muduva nyangana covers an area of over  600 square kilometers and is constantly  patrolled  despite the hunting the main aim here is  to protect the wild animals as well as  namibia the kaza program also covers  areas of botswana angola zambia and  zimbabwe  the countries have also agreed to leave  transnational migration routes open for  wildlife  casa also encompasses the ocovango delta  a unesco world heritage site in northern  botswana  the largest tributary is the kavango  river that flows from angola through  northeastern namibia and into the delta  where it forms a rich biodiverse wetland  the kavango always carries water so it's  of huge importance to the people here  in the dry season there's often a  shortage of water because the smaller  rivers dry up  and residents fear that water could  become even scarcer because canadian  company recon africa has just begun test  drilling here for oil and gas the  namibian government has a 10 stake in it  and not everything is above board  legally speaking  an environmental report was only  submitted after the fact and the  resident's concerns were not taken into  account  neither recon africa nor the government  were willing to talk to deutsche welle  test drilling has so far only taken  place in the muduva nyangana conservancy  a lot of water has been used in the  process and residents are now worried  that the drilling might have  contaminated their water supply  pressured by the community recon africa  did take water samples  but six months later there are still no  results  there is a lot of mistrust towards the  drilling company  we want an independent company to come  and test the water  some pollution in the water that's  related to their activity  and isn't telling us about it  it would be better if an independent  company came and did the tests  however environmental activists and the  affected communities have enjoyed some  success they joined forces and their  voices have been heard but still too  little has changed says max muyamuruku  and he fears there will be far-reaching  consequences  if the oil production will start  a  will start in the the kavango area  we are also going to  step in the footprint for  in nigeria where they are suffering  because of pollution from oil  or pollution from gas and the  environment will be  destroyed the air pollution also will be  destroyed  the air will be polluted the underground  water the river everything will be  contaminated  many residents believe they can profit  far more from nature conservation and  tourism than from oil  the outbreak of the virus we're dealing  with now has had a detrimental effect on  tourism because hardly any visitors come  here  before the pandemic we were at least  surviving because we were getting money  from the conservancy  and the program had great plans for the  future everyone here might soon be  getting regular payment from the program  rangers and communities plan to keep  fighting oil exploration in the country  max muyamburuku believes they need to  put a stop to it once and for all it's  the only way in the long run to protect  the unique nature of kavango  you
 

